-- title:
--   Jensen's inequality for the difference of two cube averages
-- statement:
--   Fix a dimension $d$ and a side length $h > 0$, and let $f : \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}$ be measurable and locally integrable with respect to Lebesgue measure. For $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$ write
--   $$\bar A_h(x) \;=\; \{\,u : \|u - x\|_\infty \le h/2\,\}$$
--   for the closed cube of side $h$ centred at $x$ (a set of Lebesgue measure $h^d$), and let
--   $$f_h(x) \;=\; \frac{1}{|\bar A_h(x)|}\int_{\bar A_h(x)} f$$
--   be the average of $f$ over that cube. Then for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}^d$,
--   $$\bigl(f_h(x) - f_h(y)\bigr)^2 \;\le\; \frac{1}{h^{2d}} \int_{\bar A_h(x)\times \bar A_h(y)} \bigl(f(s) - f(t)\bigr)^2 \, d(s,t) .$$
--
--   The inequality is Jensen's inequality for the piecewise-constant (tile-averaging) approximation: the square of a difference of averages is at most the average of the squares of the differences. It is what makes a tile average of the $H^{\alpha/2}$ integrand a dominant for the discretized integrand, and hence what allows a discrete energy built from cube averages to be compared with the continuous one.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Both sides are stated in $[0,\infty]$: the left side is the image of the real square, the right side a lower Lebesgue integral over the product of the two closed cubes, with the normalising factor written as the inverse of the image of $h^{2d}$. The averaging operator is Mathlib's $\textstyle\frac{1}{|A|}\int_A$, so the hypotheses of measurability and local integrability are the ones that make it the honest mean of $f$ rather than a junk value.
